{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1203778-what-should-i-be-doing-to-prep-mage-the-ascension","id":1203778,"title":"What should I be doing to prep Mage the Ascension","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-04-06T03:37:08-07:00","id":1203778,"title":"What should I be doing to prep Mage the Ascension","content":"<span style=\"color:#1D2129\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">So I\u2019m trying to get into Mage the Ascension. I used to be in a Mage the Awakening LARP as a player, years ago but the old setting fascinated me. I have the M20 mammoth core book in print and the \u201cHow do you do that!?\u201d book in pdf. I have a lot to learn about the basic rules of the old system which are significantly different from Chronicles of Darkness, lots of little things like \u201cwhat the difference between brawl and martial arts is and why one would put points in brawl whe<span style=\"font-family:inherit\">n martial arts give you maneuvers.\u201d or \u201cWhat\u2019s the deal with spirits and what systems fuel them?\u201d or \u201cWhat unfun game breakers should I be on the look out for as opposed to fun game breakers?\u201d I ask because I expect villains to be able to plan for, say, hypothetically, players slowing down time in their house to give their enemies 128 turns for every one turn they have, or transforming them into lemons, or scrying on them and targeting them through the scrying portal, or conjuring a spirit with ridiculous stats and dropping it on their door step.</span></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#1D2129\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">But that\u2019s not what I am making this post about. What I am making this game about is prep. I have the M20 quick start where the players are part of a cabal of orphans. The quick start is very short on plans. It explains that mage is bad for \u201cmodule\u201d style games. Instead, it offers potential hooks for the bridge troll cabal. So what should I be prepping for? How do I outline this? I\u2019m still learning the rules, as I said. I do intend to have the players create their own characters and let the pregens serve as NPC\u2019s who have \u201cadopted\u201d them.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Let's say I have a week to plan. What do I do? I've run DnD games before and what I do is build a plot outline, stat out some characters, build encounters relative to the player's levels, decide things that happen to entice the players and what happens if they do not interfere with the dastardly villain's plot, lead them to a climax, and decide the treasure. That's not appropriate for Mage, so what is your process for a white wolf game?</span><br /> </span></span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24086-terratorment","name":"TerraTorment","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-06T07:44:35-07:00","id":1203826,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#1D2129\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Let's say I have a week to plan. What do I do? I've run DnD games before and what I do is build a plot outline, stat out some characters, build encounters relative to the player's levels, decide things that happen to entice the players and what happens if they do not interfere with the dastardly villain's plot, lead them to a climax, and decide the treasure. That's not appropriate for Mage, so what is your process for a white wolf game?</span></span></span> </div> </div> </div>Why not?<br /> <br /> While there's no levels per se in Mage and the potential of the characters it's almost formless, there's certainly dastardly villains, locations and NPC.<br /> <br /> One thing I've read about Mage in an ST handbook somewhere it's that mages are part storytellers too. Unlike D&amp;D, players have a lot of leeway (granted, under ST supervision) to determine what their characters can and can't do. A wise ST would do well to concede this personal narrative and encourage creative solutions, otherwise you lose the few advantagers of having vague and open ended rules.<br /> <br /> But appart from that a Mage story isn't different from a Vampire story or even a D&amp;D one. Your players want to do stuff with their characters, so you make villans and shiny things to attract them and hope they don't choose to leave the country instead. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Just don't expect to have a neat list of random encounters appropriate to each power level :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-06-2018, 10:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-06T07:48:29-07:00","id":1203828,"title":"","content":"When planning an adventure, remember the old adage: no plan survives contact with the party.<br /> <br /> A set of players will find plenty of ways to circumvent or deconstruct GM plans at the best of times, often purely by accident. When working in ignorance, players will often find ways to tackle problems the GM would never have thought of. None of this is malicious, but rather the inevitable result of collaborative gaming.<br /> <br /> As such, be modular and open-minded. If characters decide to come up with some absurd plan to address some obstacle, let them try it (so long as it's logically consistent). Maybe they'll find out, given how the environment or characters are set up, their plan just isn't applicable. Or maybe they make a grievous error, landing them in trouble. Or maybe they'll solve the problem in an inventive way. If this method happens to get around any obstacles you had planned, don't worry about it. Reward the players for cleverness, and file away whatever you had planned, to be repurposed later (hopefully with any gaps filled in). Ultimately the players make the stories as much as you do.<br /> <br /> <br /> For NPCs - especially antagonists - focus less on the plot you want to tell about them, and more on what their goals are. What does the character want, and how will they accomplish it? Assuming the PCs never get involved, what stages do the NPCs go through in their overall strategy, and how could this affect the environment around them? Make these goals broad, if applicable, and make them pursue them in small chunks where feasible. That way, the PCs can afford to not find or know about the character for a length of time, without it hugely affecting the game.<br /> <br /> (For instance, an Infernalist needs to make human sacrifices. Instead of one huge sacrifice that summons a demon to earth, make it a monthly tithe of blood the Infernalist must give, in return for staying alive and in the demon's good graces. He's a threat and menace, yes, but the only thing that changes without PC involvement is an incrementing body count. It also means that any encounters planned with him can be used whenever, since his MO hardly changes from month to month.)<br /> <br /> <br /> When setting up the locale the PCs run around in, you'll need a good grasp of major NPCs they'll encounter, as well as any features of the environment itself. (If you've got a haunted house, for instance, you'll want to have all the stuff in the house, as well as a few NPCs (like neighbors or former tenants).)<br /> <br /> What you need to remember, however, is that Mages have advanced senses. Nine whole Spheres that, just at base, each give their own sensory data. Assume that players will be casting sensory effects all the time. This also means that you must not only figure out characters and environments from a mundane perspective, but also on multiple layers of reality. If they have an encounter in an area, what would the characters see if they looked forward or backward in time? Ask yourself what kind of spirits would be hanging around a given location. With spirits, it usually comes down to either ghosts, nature spirits (fire, smoke, electricity, animals), or spirits of concepts (knowledge, hate, forgetfulness). Take a look at what the environment is, and what themes are associated with it; that'll give you a good starting point.<br /> <br /> Don't feel like you have to massively over prepare for this, though. Rather, decide in advance what sort of information mystical senses would pick up that is <i>relevant</i>. Not every building will have a ghost hanging around, not every time period immediately before (or after) when you're there will be at all interesting. Not every heat signature or electricity flow is important, and not every room harbors secret doors or unusual materials. And naturally, you'll want to focus on the Spheres the characters actually have. If no one has Spirit or Time, you don't need to bother figuring out what spirits are about or what exact order of events took place.<br /> <br /> Why is sensory stuff even important? Because it gives PCs avenues for gathering clues and solving problems. It's probably a good idea to make sure that if characters NEED to know something, there be multiple ways to learn it (or to realize they need to study further). If a room has a secret door, Matter 1 could sense it. But so could Forces 1, by reading how air flows and spotting an irregularity. Correspondence 1 might spot it, when the character gains an detailed view of 3D space. As could Entropy 1, if the character spots weakness in the wall that implies a seam. Time 2 could be used to look back (or forward) until the character sees the hidden door in use. Spirit 2 could be employed to talk to any spirit present, who might mention the passage.<br /> <br /> And all of that not even getting into the characters noticing it through just Perception + Alertness/Investigation rolls. Note, also, that just having a Sphere can allow a character to get more from their mundane sense rolls, as their familiarity with a certain aspect of Reality allows them to gather information other characters couldn't. The point here is that redundancy is your friend.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-06T07:56:22-07:00","id":1203832,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1203778-what-should-i-be-doing-to-prep-mage-the-ascension?p=1203826#post1203826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing I've read about Mage in an ST handbook somewhere it's that mages are part storytellers too. Unlike D&amp;D, players have a lot of leeway (granted, under ST supervision) to determine what their characters can and can't do. A wise ST would do well to concede this personal narrative and encourage creative solutions, otherwise you lose the few advantagers of having vague and open ended rules.<br /> <br /> But appart from that a Mage story isn't different from a Vampire story or even a D&amp;D one. Your players want to do stuff with their characters, so you make villans and shiny things to attract them and hope they don't choose to leave the country instead. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Adding to this, take some time when the players are creating their characters to help them flesh out what their characters' goals are. Why is your mage going on &quot;adventures&quot; (for whatever that means), rather than staying in their Sanctum and studying privately for a hundred years? What does the character want to DO with their unfathomable cosmic powers? What conflicts are baked into their very character concepts? Once your players can answer these questions, you as the Storyteller will have an easier time tailoring the gaming experience to them. There's only so long the party can be forced into action by circumstance, before the characters would rather leave or bow out. Players find stories more compelling if they, themselves, drive the plot through their wants, desires, and actions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}